//? Title: Backward Euler: Stability Bought With an Equation Solve
//? Subtitle: Stability functions, first-order convergence, a stiff heat equation and Robertson's chemical kinetics, with datamunge's algorithms::backward_euler and algorithms::backward_euler_adaptive
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Forward Euler steps along the slope at the start of each step; backward Euler uses the slope at
the end, \(y_{n+1}=y_n+h\,f(t_{n+1},y_{n+1})\), so every step means solving an equation. We derive
what that buys. On \(y'=\lambda y\) the method multiplies by \(R(z)=1/(1-z)\) with \(z=h\lambda\),
which has magnitude below 1 on the whole left half-plane (A-stability) and tends to 0 as
\(z\to-\infty\) (L-stability). Its local error is \(-\tfrac{h^2}{2}y''\), the mirror image of forward
Euler's, and a convergence study confirms first order with errors of equal size and opposite sign.
On the heat equation discretised on 49 interior points, forward Euler needs steps below
\(2\times10^{-4}\) and explodes just above that, while backward Euler is stable with steps 50 times
larger. On Robertson's stiff chemical kinetics, backward Euler with step-doubling error control
integrates to \(t=4\times10^{10}\) in 738 steps at a relative tolerance of \(10^{-3}\), where forward Euler would need about
\(2\times10^{14}\).
)

\toc

> The method

>> An implicit step

Integrating \(y'=f(t,y)\) over one step and approximating the integral by its right endpoint gives

$$
y_{n+1}=y_n+h\,f(t_{n+1},y_{n+1}).
$$
\alttext(y n plus 1 equals y n plus h times f of t n plus 1 and y n plus 1.)

The unknown appears on both sides. `backward_euler()` solves \(G(Y)=Y-y_n-h\,f(t_{n+1},Y)=0\) by
Newton's method,

$$
\bigl(I-h\,J_f(Y^{(k)})\bigr)\,\Delta=-G(Y^{(k)}),\qquad Y^{(k+1)}=Y^{(k)}+\Delta ,
$$
\alttext(I minus h times the Jacobian of f at Y k, times Delta, equals minus G of Y k; Y k plus 1 equals Y k plus Delta.)

starting from the forward Euler value and building \(J_f\) by central differences. For a linear
\(f\), one Newton iteration solves the step exactly. Each step therefore costs a Jacobian and a
linear solve, \(O(d^3)\) for a system of size \(d\), against one evaluation of \(f\) for forward Euler.

>> Local error

Expand the exact solution about \(t_{n+1}\): \(y(t_n)=y(t_{n+1})-h\,y'(t_{n+1})+\tfrac{h^2}2y''+O(h^3)\).
So the exact solution satisfies the scheme up to

$$
y(t_{n+1})-y(t_n)-h\,f\bigl(t_{n+1},y(t_{n+1})\bigr)=-\frac{h^2}{2}\,y''+O(h^3),
$$
\alttext(y at t n plus 1, minus y at t n, minus h f at t n plus 1, equals minus h squared over 2 times y double prime, plus order h cubed.)

while forward Euler's local error is \(+\tfrac{h^2}{2}y''\). Summing about \(1/h\) local errors gives a
global error of order \(h\) for both, with leading terms of opposite sign. Averaging the two slopes
cancels them; that is the trapezoidal rule, which is second order.

>> Stability

Apply a one-step method to the test equation \(y'=\lambda y\), \(\mathrm{Re}\,\lambda<0\), whose
solutions decay. Each step multiplies \(y_n\) by a *stability function* of \(z=h\lambda\):

$$
R_{\text{FE}}(z)=1+z,\qquad R_{\text{BE}}(z)=\frac1{1-z},\qquad R_{\text{TR}}(z)=\frac{1+z/2}{1-z/2}.
$$
\alttext(R for forward Euler is 1 plus z. R for backward Euler is 1 over 1 minus z. R for the trapezoidal rule is 1 plus z over 2, over 1 minus z over 2.)

The numerical solution decays when \(|R(z)|<1\). For backward Euler, \(|1-z|\ge1-\mathrm{Re}\,z>1\)
whenever \(\mathrm{Re}\,z<0\), so it decays for *every* step size: the method is *A-stable*. Forward
Euler needs \(|1+z|<1\), a disc of radius 1 centred at \(-1\); for real \(\lambda<0\) that means
\(h<2/|\lambda|\). The trapezoidal rule is also A-stable, but \(R_{\text{TR}}(z)\to-1\) as
\(z\to-\infty\), so very fast modes are not damped and flip sign each step. Backward Euler's
\(R(z)\to0\): fast modes are wiped out in a single step. That stronger property is
*L-stability*. `stability_function()` evaluates these functions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/backward_euler_stability.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  using M = alg::OdeMethod;
  std::print("| \\(z=h\\lambda\\) | \\(|R_\\text{{FE}}|\\) | \\(|R_\\text{{BE}}|\\) | \\(R_\\text{{TR}}\\) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (double z : {-0.1, -1.0, -1.9, -2.1, -10.0, -100.0, -1e4})
    std::print("| {} | {:.4g} | {:.4g} | {:.4g} |\n", z, std::abs(alg::stability_function(M::ForwardEuler, z)),
               std::abs(alg::stability_function(M::BackwardEuler, z)), alg::stability_function(M::Trapezoidal, z).real());

  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 60; ++i) xs.push_back(-4.0 + 7.0 * i / 60);
  for (int j = 0; j <= 60; ++j) ys.push_back(-3.5 + 7.0 * j / 60);
  auto panel = [&](M method, const char* title) {
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(xs.size(), std::vector<double>(ys.size()));
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < xs.size(); ++i)
      for (std::size_t j = 0; j < ys.size(); ++j)
        z[i][j] = std::abs(alg::stability_function(method, {xs[i], ys[j]})) <= 1.0 ? 1.0 : std::nan("");
    return dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
        .image(xs, ys, z, {dm::RGB{147, 197, 253}}, 0.0, 1.0)
        .abline_v(0.0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
        .abline_h(0.0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
        .x_limits(-4, 3)
        .y_limits(-3.5, 3.5)
        .title(title)
        .x_label("Re z")
        .y_label("Im z");
  };
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 3)
      .add(panel(M::ForwardEuler, "Forward Euler"))
      .add(panel(M::BackwardEuler, "Backward Euler"))
      .add(panel(M::Trapezoidal, "Trapezoidal"))
      .size(1200, 440)
      .save_svg("build/backward_euler_stability.svg");
}
```
