| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| -0.1 | 0.9 | 0.9091 | 0.9048 |
| -1 | 0 | 0.5 | 0.3333 |
| -1.9 | 0.9 | 0.3448 | 0.02564 |
| -2.1 | 1.1 | 0.3226 | -0.02439 |
| -10 | 9 | 0.09091 | -0.6667 |
| -100 | 99 | 0.009901 | -0.9608 |
| -10000 | 9999 | 9.999e-05 | -0.9996 |
\image(figures/backward_euler_stability.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Stability regions \(|R(z)|\le1\) (shaded) in the complex plane of \(z=h\lambda\), for forward Euler, backward Euler and the trapezoidal rule.)
\alttext(Three panels of the complex plane from minus 4 to 3 across and minus 3.5 to 3.5 up. Forward Euler's stable region is a small disc of radius 1 centred at minus 1. Backward Euler's covers everything except a disc of radius 1 centred at plus 1. The trapezoidal rule's is exactly the left half-plane.)

The shaded regions match the derivation: forward Euler's is the disc \(|1+z|\le1\), backward
Euler's is everything outside the disc \(|1-z|<1\) (including much of the right half-plane, where
it damps solutions that should grow), and the trapezoidal rule's is exactly the left half-plane.
The table shows the difference on the negative real axis. Forward Euler's factor passes 1 at
\(z=-2\) and then grows without bound. Backward Euler's shrinks like \(1/|z|\), to \(10^{-4}\) at
\(z=-10^4\). The trapezoidal factor stays below 1 in size but approaches \(-1\): at \(z=-10^4\) a
fast mode loses only 0.04% of its amplitude per step and changes sign every step.

> First-order convergence

The test problem \(y'=y-t^2+1\), \(y(0)=0.5\) on \([0,2]\) has the exact solution
\(y(t)=(t+1)^2-\tfrac12e^t\), with \(y(2)=9-\tfrac12e^2\). The block solves it with both Euler methods
for step counts from 10 to 10,240.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/backward_euler_convergence.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto f = [](double t, const std::vector<double>& y) { return std::vector<double>{y[0] - t * t + 1.0}; };
  const double exact = 9.0 - 0.5 * std::exp(2.0);
  std::print("| steps | \\(h\\) | forward error | backward error | ratio | average error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> lh, lfe, lbe;
  double previous = 0;
  for (int steps = 10; steps <= 10240; steps *= 2) {
    const double fe = alg::euler_method(f, {0.5}, 0.0, 2.0, steps).y.back()[0] - exact;
    const double be = alg::backward_euler(f, {0.5}, 0.0, 2.0, steps).y.back()[0] - exact;
    std::print("| {} | {:.3g} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} | {} | {:.2e} |\n", steps, 2.0 / steps, fe, be,
               previous ? std::format("{:.3f}", previous / be) : std::string("–"), 0.5 * (fe + be));
    previous = be;
    lh.push_back(std::log10(2.0 / steps));
    lfe.push_back(std::log10(std::abs(fe)));
    lbe.push_back(std::log10(std::abs(be)));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(lh, lfe, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
      .points(lh, lfe, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 6.0)
      .lines(lh, lbe, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
      .points(lh, lbe, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 4.0)
      .legend({"forward Euler", "backward Euler"}, {dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}})
      .y_limits(-4, 1)
      .title("Error at t = 2 against step size")
      .x_label("log10 h")
      .y_label("log10 |error|")
      .size(800, 480)
      .save_svg("build/backward_euler_convergence.svg");
}
```
