| 320 | 0.00625 | -1.672e-02 | 1.696e-02 | 2.015 | 1.20e-04 |
| 640 | 0.00313 | -8.390e-03 | 8.451e-03 | 2.007 | 3.01e-05 |
| 1280 | 0.00156 | -4.203e-03 | 4.218e-03 | 2.004 | 7.52e-06 |
| 2560 | 0.000781 | -2.103e-03 | 2.107e-03 | 2.002 | 1.88e-06 |
| 5120 | 0.000391 | -1.052e-03 | 1.053e-03 | 2.001 | 4.70e-07 |
| 10240 | 0.000195 | -5.262e-04 | 5.264e-04 | 2.000 | 1.17e-07 |
\image(figures/backward_euler_convergence.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Absolute error at \(t=2\) against the step size, on log scales, for forward Euler (red) and backward Euler (blue).)
\alttext(Log-log plot of absolute error against step size for both methods. Both lines are straight with slope 1 and lie on top of each other for small steps; for the largest steps backward Euler's error is slightly larger.)

Both methods are first order: halving \(h\) halves the error, and the ratio of successive
backward Euler errors approaches 2.000. The errors have opposite signs, forward Euler low and
backward Euler high, and nearly equal sizes, as the local-error expansion predicts. Their average
has an error that falls by a factor of 4 per halving, from \(1.3\times10^{-1}\) to
\(1.2\times10^{-7}\): the average behaves like a second-order method, which is the trapezoidal
rule's advantage. On this non-stiff problem backward Euler gains nothing over forward Euler and
costs a Newton solve per step.

> A stiff problem: the heat equation

The heat equation \(u_t=u_{xx}\) on \([0,1]\) with \(u=0\) at both ends, discretised by central
differences on \(N=49\) interior points (\(\Delta x=0.02\)), becomes the linear system
\(u'=Au\) with \((Au)_i=(u_{i-1}-2u_i+u_{i+1})/\Delta x^2\). Its eigenvalues are

$$
\lambda_k=-\frac{4}{\Delta x^2}\sin^2\frac{k\pi\Delta x}{2},\qquad k=1,\dots,N,
$$
\alttext(lambda k equals minus 4 over delta x squared times sine squared of k pi delta x over 2, for k from 1 to N.)

from \(\lambda_1\approx-\pi^2\approx-9.87\) to \(\lambda_{49}\approx-4/\Delta x^2=-9990\). The ratio of
1,000 between the fastest and slowest decay rates is what makes the system *stiff*. Forward Euler
must keep \(h|\lambda_{49}|<2\), so \(h<2.002\times10^{-4}\), even though the solution worth
computing changes on a time scale of \(1/\pi^2\approx0.1\). The initial condition
\(u_0=\sin\pi x+\sin5\pi x\) has the exact solution
\(u=e^{-\pi^2t}\sin\pi x+e^{-25\pi^2t}\sin5\pi x\). The block integrates to \(t=0.1\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/backward_euler_heat.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const int N = 49;
  const double dx = 1.0 / (N + 1), pi = std::numbers::pi, T = 0.1;
  const auto heat = [&](double, const std::vector<double>& u) {
    std::vector<double> du(N);
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i)
      du[i] = ((i > 0 ? u[i - 1] : 0.0) - 2 * u[i] + (i + 1 < N ? u[i + 1] : 0.0)) / (dx * dx);
    return du;
  };
  std::vector<double> x(N), u0(N), exact(N);
  for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    x[i] = (i + 1) * dx;
    u0[i] = std::sin(pi * x[i]) + std::sin(5 * pi * x[i]);
    exact[i] = std::exp(-pi * pi * T) * std::sin(pi * x[i]) + std::exp(-25 * pi * pi * T) * std::sin(5 * pi * x[i]);
  }
  auto max_error = [&](const std::vector<double>& u) {
    double e = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) e = std::max(e, std::abs(u[i] - exact[i]));
    return e;
  };
  std::print("Fastest eigenvalue: {:.1f}; forward Euler limit h < {:.4e}.\n\n", -4 / (dx * dx) * std::pow(std::sin(N * pi * dx / 2), 2),
             2 / (4 / (dx * dx) * std::pow(std::sin(N * pi * dx / 2), 2)));
  std::print("| method | \\(h\\) | steps | \\(h\\lvert\\lambda_{{49}}\\rvert\\) | max error at \\(t=0.1\\) | ms |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  struct Run {
    const char* name;
    bool implicit;
    int steps;
  };
  std::vector<double> fe_bad, be_big;
  for (const Run r : {Run{"forward Euler", false, 1000}, Run{"forward Euler", false, 500}, Run{"forward Euler", false, 490},
                      Run{"forward Euler", false, 480}, Run{"forward Euler", false, 400}, Run{"backward Euler", true, 1000}, Run{"backward Euler", true, 100},
                      Run{"backward Euler", true, 10}}) {
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    const auto sol = r.implicit ? alg::backward_euler(heat, u0, 0.0, T, r.steps) : alg::euler_method(heat, u0, 0.0, T, r.steps);
    const double ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
    const double h = T / r.steps;
    std::print("| {} | {:.3g} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3e} | {:.1f} |\n", r.name, h, r.steps, h * 4 / (dx * dx) * std::pow(std::sin(N * pi * dx / 2), 2),
               max_error(sol.y.back()), ms);
    if (!r.implicit && r.steps == 480) fe_bad = sol.y.back();
    if (r.implicit && r.steps == 10) be_big = sol.y.back();
  }
  std::vector<double> xb{0.0}, eb{0.0}, bb{0.0}, fb{0.0}, ub{0.0};
  for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    xb.push_back(x[i]);
    eb.push_back(exact[i]);
    bb.push_back(be_big[i]);
    fb.push_back(fe_bad[i]);
    ub.push_back(u0[i]);
  }
  xb.push_back(1.0);
  for (auto* v : {&eb, &bb, &fb, &ub}) v->push_back(0.0);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(xb, ub, "", dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, 2.0)
      .lines(xb, fb, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5)
      .lines(xb, eb, "", dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 3.0)
      .points(xb, bb, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .legend({"t = 0", "FE, h = 2.08e-4", "exact", "BE, h = 0.01"},
              {dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}})
      .y_limits(-1.6, 2.6)
      .title("Heat equation at t = 0.1")
      .x_label("x")
      .y_label("u")
      .size(900, 520)
      .save_svg("build/backward_euler_heat.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Fastest eigenvalue: -9990.1; forward Euler limit h < 2.0020e-04.

| method | \(h\) | steps | \(h\lvert\lambda_{49}\rvert\) | max error at \(t=0.1\) | ms |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| forward Euler | 0.0001 | 1000 | 0.999 | 6.052e-05 | 0.2 |
| forward Euler | 0.0002 | 500 | 1.998 | 2.422e-04 | 0.0 |
| forward Euler | 0.000204 | 490 | 2.039 | 2.496e-04 | 0.0 |
| forward Euler | 0.000208 | 480 | 2.081 | 3.624e-01 | 0.0 |
| forward Euler | 0.00025 | 400 | 2.498 | 3.848e+53 | 0.0 |
| backward Euler | 0.0001 | 1000 | 0.999 | 3.024e-04 | 22.9 |
| backward Euler | 0.001 | 100 | 9.990 | 1.928e-03 | 2.7 |
| backward Euler | 0.01 | 10 | 99.901 | 1.756e-02 | 0.4 |
\image(figures/backward_euler_heat.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Temperature profiles: the initial condition (grey), the exact solution at \(t=0.1\) (black), backward Euler with 10 steps of \(h=0.01\) (blue points), and forward Euler with 480 steps of \(h=2.08\times10^{-4}\), 4% above its stability limit (red).)
\alttext(Plot of u against x from 0 to 1. The grey initial condition has a large central hump and two side humps. The black exact solution at t equals 0.1 is a single low arch peaking near 0.37. Blue backward Euler points sit on it. The red forward Euler curve follows the arch but with a zig-zag between neighbouring points of amplitude up to about 0.36.)

The stability limit is sharp. Forward Euler with \(h=2\times10^{-4}\) (\(h|\lambda_{49}|=1.998\)) is
accurate to \(2.4\times10^{-4}\), and at \(h=2.04\times10^{-4}\) the unstable mode still has not
grown from rounding level to anything visible in 490 steps. At \(h=2.08\times10^{-4}\) it has: the
fastest mode, an alternating zig-zag between grid points with amplitude factor
\(|1-2.081|=1.081\) per step, grows from about \(10^{-16}\) to 0.36 in 480 steps. At \(h=2.5\times10^{-4}\)
the error is \(4\times10^{53}\). None of this mode is in the initial data; rounding alone seeds it.

Backward Euler has no such limit. With 10 steps of \(h=0.01\), a hundred times past forward
Euler's limit, the error is \(1.8\times10^{-2}\), and the fast modes are damped by a factor of
about 100 per step rather than amplified. Its error is set by accuracy, not stability:
it falls in proportion to \(h\), and at equal step size it is larger than forward Euler's. The slow mode \(e^{-\pi^2t}\) is
approximated by \((1+h\pi^2)^{-n}\), which decays too slowly, and so does the spatially discretised
mode (its rate is \(\pi^2(1-\pi^2\Delta x^2/12)\)); forward Euler's \((1-h\pi^2)^n\) decays too fast
and partly cancels the spatial error.
Each implicit step here builds a \(49\times49\) Jacobian by differences, so per step it costs far
more; a production code would pass the (tridiagonal) Jacobian directly.

> Robertson's chemical kinetics

Robertson's reaction system (1966) is the classic stiff test problem: three species with rate
constants spanning nine orders of magnitude,

$$
\begin{aligned}
y_1'&=-0.04\,y_1+10^4\,y_2y_3,\\
y_2'&=\phantom{-}0.04\,y_1-10^4\,y_2y_3-3\times10^7\,y_2^2,\\
y_3'&=3\times10^7\,y_2^2,
\end{aligned}\qquad y(0)=(1,0,0).
$$
\alttext(y 1 prime equals minus 0.04 y 1 plus 10 to the 4 y 2 y 3. y 2 prime equals 0.04 y 1 minus 10 to the 4 y 2 y 3 minus 3 times 10 to the 7 y 2 squared. y 3 prime equals 3 times 10 to the 7 y 2 squared. Initial value 1, 0, 0.)

The right-hand sides sum to zero, so \(y_1+y_2+y_3=1\) for all time. The intermediate \(y_2\) is
created slowly and destroyed fast, so it settles within about \(10^{-2}\) time units onto a
*slow manifold* where it stays tiny, and the solution then evolves on time scales up to
\(10^{10}\). Linearising, the fast eigenvalue is about \(-(10^4y_3+6\times10^7y_2)\), close to
\(-10^4\) once \(y_3\approx1\). Forward Euler would therefore need \(h<2\times10^{-4}\) for the whole
run: \(2\times10^{14}\) steps to reach \(t=4\times10^{10}\).

`backward_euler_adaptive()` controls the step by *step doubling*: it takes one step of size \(h\)
and two of size \(h/2\). Their difference is about \(\tfrac{h^2}{4}y''\), an estimate of the local
error of the half steps, and since that error is \(O(h^2)\), the next step is scaled by
\(0.9/\sqrt{\text{err}}\). The block runs three relative tolerances with \(10^{-14}\) as the
absolute tolerance, since \(y_2\) falls to \(10^{-13}\), and compares with reference values
computed by a fifth-order Radau method at a relative tolerance of \(10^{-12}\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/backward_euler_robertson.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto robertson = [](double, const std::vector<double>& y) {
    return std::vector<double>{-0.04 * y[0] + 1e4 * y[1] * y[2], 0.04 * y[0] - 1e4 * y[1] * y[2] - 3e7 * y[1] * y[1],
                               3e7 * y[1] * y[1]};
  };
  // Reference solution (SciPy Radau, rtol 1e-12) at t = 0.4, 4e2, 4e5, 4e10.
  const double ref_t[] = {0.4, 400.0, 4e5, 4e10};
  const double ref_y1[] = {0.9851721138609921, 0.45051866847111177, 0.004938274520980084, 5.208345176317862e-08};

  std::print("| rtol | accepted | rejected | \\(f\\) calls | Newton iterations | ms | \\(y_1\\) rel. error at 0.4 | at 400 | at \\(4\\times10^5\\) | at \\(4\\times10^{{10}}\\) | \\(\\max\\lvert\\sum y-1\\rvert\\) |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  alg::AdaptiveOdeResult keep;
  for (double rtol : {1e-2, 1e-3, 1e-4}) {
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    auto r = alg::backward_euler_adaptive(robertson, {1.0, 0.0, 0.0}, 0.0, 4e10, rtol, 1e-14, 1e-6);
    const double ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
    std::string errs;
    for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) {
      // Interpolate y1 in log t between the accepted points around ref_t[k].
      const auto& t = r.solution.t;
      std::size_t i = std::upper_bound(t.begin(), t.end(), ref_t[k]) - t.begin();
      i = std::min(i, t.size() - 1);
      const double w = (std::log(ref_t[k]) - std::log(t[i - 1])) / (std::log(t[i]) - std::log(t[i - 1]));
      const double y1 = std::exp((1 - w) * std::log(r.solution.y[i - 1][0]) + w * std::log(r.solution.y[i][0]));
      errs += std::format(" {:.1e} |", (y1 - ref_y1[k]) / ref_y1[k]);
    }
    double drift = 0;
    for (const auto& y : r.solution.y) drift = std::max(drift, std::abs(y[0] + y[1] + y[2] - 1.0));
    std::print("| {:g} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.1f} |{} {:.1e} |\n", rtol, r.accepted, r.rejected, r.f_evaluations,
               r.newton_iterations, ms, errs, drift);
    if (rtol == 1e-3) keep = std::move(r);
  }

  std::vector<double> lt, y1, y2, y3, lh;
  for (std::size_t i = 1; i < keep.solution.t.size(); ++i) {
    lt.push_back(std::log10(keep.solution.t[i]));
    y1.push_back(keep.solution.y[i][0]);
    y2.push_back(1e4 * keep.solution.y[i][1]);
    y3.push_back(keep.solution.y[i][2]);
    lh.push_back(std::log10(keep.step_sizes[i - 1]));
  }
  auto species = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                     .lines(lt, y1, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0)
                     .lines(lt, y2, "", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 2.0)
                     .lines(lt, y3, "", dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}, 2.0)
                     .legend({"y1", "1e4 · y2", "y3"}, {dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{22, 163, 74}})
                     .y_limits(0, 1.25)
                     .title("Concentrations (rtol 1e-3)")
                     .x_label("log10 t")
                     .y_label("concentration");
  auto steps = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                   .points(lt, lh, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.0)
                   .abline(std::log10(1.0), 1.0, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0)
                   .abline_h(std::log10(2e-4), dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.0)
                   .y_limits(-7, 11)
                   .title("Accepted step sizes")
                   .x_label("log10 t")
                   .y_label("log10 h");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(species).add(steps).size(1200, 480).save_svg("build/backward_euler_robertson.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| rtol | accepted | rejected | \(f\) calls | Newton iterations | ms | \(y_1\) rel. error at 0.4 | at 400 | at \(4\times10^5\) | at \(4\times10^{10}\) | \(\max\lvert\sum y-1\rvert\) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.01 | 254 | 3 | 15939 | 2111 | 0.7 | -1.4e-04 | 1.5e-02 | 6.1e-02 | 6.4e-02 | 6.7e-16 |
| 0.001 | 738 | 4 | 39057 | 5075 | 1.5 | 3.8e-05 | 5.3e-03 | 1.9e-02 | 2.0e-02 | 2.2e-15 |
| 0.0001 | 2207 | 4 | 98819 | 12427 | 4.2 | 1.4e-05 | 1.7e-03 | 6.1e-03 | 6.4e-03 | 2.1e-15 |
\image(figures/backward_euler_robertson.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the three concentrations against \(\log_{10}t\), with \(y_2\) scaled by \(10^4\). Right: each accepted step size; the grey line is \(h=t\) and the red line forward Euler's stability limit \(2\times10^{-4}\).)
\alttext(Left panel: concentrations against log10 t from minus 6 to 10.6. y1 falls from 1 to 0 between t of about 1 and 10 to the 5; y3 rises correspondingly; y2 times 10 to the 4 rises to a plateau of about 0.37 by t of about 3 times 10 to the minus 3 and then decays. Right panel: log10 of each accepted step against log10 t. Steps start near 10 to the minus 6, dip near t equals 10 to the minus 5, then grow roughly in proportion to t up to about 10 to the 9, far above the red line at forward Euler's limit of 2 times 10 to the minus 4.)

The adaptive integration reaches \(t=4\times10^{10}\) in 254, 738 and 2,207 accepted steps at
relative tolerances of \(10^{-2}\), \(10^{-3}\) and \(10^{-4}\), with only 3 or 4 rejected steps
each and a few milliseconds of time. Tightening the tolerance tenfold multiplies the steps by
about 3, close to \(\sqrt{10}\), as an \(O(h^2)\) local error predicts. The conservation law
\(y_1+y_2+y_3=1\) holds to \(2\times10^{-15}\): it is linear, and both the Newton iteration and
the difference Jacobian respect linear invariants exactly, up to rounding.

The accuracy follows the tolerance too, but the error accumulates: at \(t=0.4\) the relative error
in \(y_1\) is at most \(1.4\times10^{-4}\), while at \(4\times10^{10}\) it is 6.4%, 2.0% and 0.64% for the three
tolerances. That is the price of a first-order method controlled by local error: higher-order
stiff solvers (BDF, Radau) reach the same accuracy in far fewer steps.

The step-size plot shows the method's character. During the initial transient, while \(y_2\) rises
to its plateau, steps stay below about \(3\times10^{-5}\). Once the solution is on the slow manifold the step
grows roughly in proportion to \(t\), crossing forward Euler's limit of \(2\times10^{-4}\) before
\(t=10^{-2}\) and reaching \(10^9\) by the end, thirteen orders of magnitude past it. The fast
eigenvalue is still there, but backward Euler damps it at every step instead of resolving it.

> Summary

- Backward Euler's stability function \(1/(1-z)\) is below 1 in size on the whole left half-plane
  and tends to 0 for very stiff modes: it is A-stable and L-stable. The trapezoidal rule is
  A-stable but lets stiff modes ring with factor near \(-1\).
- Its local error is \(-\tfrac{h^2}2y''\), the negative of forward Euler's; both converge at first
  order with nearly equal and opposite errors.
- On the heat equation with 49 points, forward Euler fails sharply just above \(h=2.0\times10^{-4}\);
  backward Euler is stable at \(h=0.01\), with accuracy, not stability, limiting the step.
- On Robertson's kinetics, step-doubling control lets backward Euler take steps up to \(10^9\) and
  reach \(t=4\times10^{10}\) in a few hundred steps, against \(2\times10^{14}\) for forward Euler,
  with accuracy that tightens as \(\sqrt{\text{tol}}\) in cost.
